## STEP 1 — DELTA

NO_DELTA — CloudFront fronting of box/`+`/`login/` and api/status reconfirmed; `/status` body and zero-header posture unchanged. No new paths, no new headers, no new behaviors across all probe cycles.

## STEP 2 — PRIORITIZE

[PRIO] `box.signageos.io/status` — 6.65 (attack=7, business=8, tech=4, gate=10, cloud=6, fresh=2)
[PRIO] `box.signageos.io` CORS/CSP — 5.65 (attack=6, business=6, tech=5, gate=10, cloud=3, fresh=1)
[PRIO] `api.signageos.io/v1/organization/{uid}/security-token` — 6.70 (attack=9, business=9, tech=8, gate=2, cloud=4, fresh=3) — off-phase (target=box)

## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] box /status unauthenticated K8s infra info-leak behind CloudFront
class: MISCONFIG
asset: box.signageos.io/status
confidence: 95
reasoning: Unauthenticated GET returns JSON with K8s pod hostname (rotating), 40-hex process UID, Node v20.20.2, and full service topology (amqp0, redis0-3, mongoDB0-3) with per-service responseTime. Zero security headers (HSTS/xfo/xcto/CSP all absent) — differential vs `/` and `/login/` which are hardened. Confirmed across 30+ probes spanning 3 days. Now fronted by CloudFront; body/headers identical.
evidence_needed: Fresh curl with headers + body with sha256 hash to PoC-package the leak behind the new CloudFront edge
verify_steps: `curl -sD /tmp/poc_box_status_h.txt --max-time 20 https://box.signageos.io/status -o /tmp/poc_box_status_b.txt && echo "---HEADERS---" && cat /tmp/poc_box_status_h.txt && echo "---BODY---" && cat /tmp/poc_box_status_b.txt && echo "---SHA256---" && sha256sum /tmp/poc_box_status_b.txt /tmp/poc_box_status_h.txt`
impact: Internal infrastructure mapping (pod naming convention, service topology, process UID, Node version) enabling targeted follow-up attacks. Medium severity — reconnaissance enabler, not direct data compromise.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] box CORS static whitelist + wildcard expands cross-origin trust boundary
class: MISCONFIG
asset: box.signageos.io/ and /login/
confidence: 55
reasoning: 17 static ACAO values including `http://box.signageos.io` (plaintext HTTP variant) and `https://*.zdusercontent.com` (wildcard). Static whitelist — spoofed Origin `evil.test` NOT reflected. No `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` on any box path. The wildcard + HTTP variant expand trust boundary but without credentials flag, browser-based credential theft is blocked.
evidence_needed: Confirm no ACAO+ACAC combination under any Origin; demonstrate wildcard is the only theoretically exploitable combination
verify_steps: `curl -sI -H "Origin: https://evil.test" https://box.signageos.io/ 2>&1 | grep -iE 'access-control'` + `curl -sI -H "Origin: https://evil.zdusercontent.com" https://box.signageos.io/ 2>&1 | grep -iE 'access-control'`
impact: Limited — no credentials flag means no cookie/auth theft. The HTTP variant is most notable but requires MITM on plaintext. Low-medium severity.
testability: PASSIVE

